    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-ground-b05-wet-soil-trafficability","source_ref":"source:static-ground-b05-usfs-trafficability","anchor":{"section":"Abstract; Soil moisture and trafficability"},"note":"USDA Forest Service synthesis identifies strong relations among soil moisture, soil strength and trafficability, and describes rutting/compaction under moist-to-wet traffic. It supplies no local soil, moisture content, load, pass count, route, rut depth or bearing value.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-ground-b05-excavation-groundwater-seepage","source_ref":"source:static-ground-b05-usace-groundwater-control","anchor":{"section":"2.1 Need for Groundwater Control"},"note":"USACE says control can intercept seepage that would otherwise emerge from slopes or bottom of an excavation and that uncontrolled groundwater can reduce stability. It supplies no local water table, soil profile, inflow rate, excavation geometry, control method or safe depth.","review_status":"approved"},
    {"schema":"world_knowledge_evidence_v1","evidence_ref":"evidence:static-ground-b05-birch-bark-composition","source_ref":"source:static-ground-b05-birch-bark-composition","anchor":{"section":"Results and discussion — Part A: experimental results"},"note":"The cited study reports suberin and lignin in its analysed Betula pendula bark. It does not establish composition for all birch bark, a current sheet, freshness, ignition, wet-fuel performance, smoke, heat output or historical availability.","review_status":"approved"},
